   "CHARACTER SHEET - LittleCrab   (bake this FIRST, before any scene)\n\n🔴 THE SHEET IS PAINTED IN THE SAME MEDIUM AS THE BOOK. Ink and gouache wash on cool\n  grey-toned toothed paper #E4E5DF, laid with a soft round brush. The brush grain must be\n  visible on the shell, the contour must be left open in places, and a darker wash must be\n  pooled along the shell edge where the paint dried. Do NOT render this crab smoothly just\n  because there is no background behind it. There is NO red anywhere on this sheet.\n\nFACE: there is no face on the shell. Two EYE-STALKS rise from the top of the carapace, each\n  ending in a small dark oval eye #2B2320. 🔴 THE EYE-STALKS ARE THE ENTIRE EXPRESSION\n  SYSTEM - the angle of the two stalks is the acting:\n    eager      = both stalks tall and tipped forward\n    indignant  = stalks pulled in close together and pushed forward like a jab\n    puzzled    = stalks splayed, each aimed somewhere different\n    waiting    = stalks level, still, both aimed at the horizon\n    joyful     = stalks curved outward and lifted\n  A tiny mouth line under the front of the shell, with small bubbles of BARE PAPER when happy.\n  No dot-eye on the shell, no blush, no glossy catchlight.\nBODY: a cool shore-crab grey-green carapace #6F7A66, a slightly warmer underside #8A8B72,\n  darker joints #4E5548. Round low shell, wider than tall, painted as ONE flat wash with the\n  darker wash pooled along the rim - matte, no plastic shine, no specular highlight, no\n  rendered chitin texture.\nCLAWS & LEGS: eight thin legs, and two front claws drawn deliberately a little oversized and\n  expressive - they point, they droop, they wave. Each leg is one or two brush strokes, not a\n  jointed anatomical study.\n🔴 NO RED, NO ORANGE, NO PINK ANYWHERE ON THIS CRAB. The red belongs to the starfish alone.\nBUILD & SILHOUETTE: a small young crab. Silhouette = round shell + two raised claws + two\n  eye-stalks, readable at thumbnail size and instantly distinct from a snail, a shrimp and a\n  starfish.\nREFERENCE SHEET: full-body from slightly above (the book's main angle) / three-quarter view\n  walking sideways with the legs mid-step / front view with both claws raised /\n  back view sitting still, seen from behind with both eye-stalks aimed away at a horizon;\n  plus FOUR eye-stalk expression close-ups: eager (stalks tall and forward),\n  indignant-accusing (one claw pointing, stalks pushed in close),\n  doubtful-waiting (stalks level and still, claws lowered and folded),\n  joyful (stalks curved, claw raised in a wave, bubbles of bare paper at the mouth).\n  Plain cool grey paper background, no scenery, no red.\nFACE SEPARATION (required): the eyes and stalks must read apart from the shell in VALUE - bare\n  paper or a tonal step where the stalks meet the carapace, never one flat mass. Test: shrink\n  this sheet to thumbnail size and the mood must still be legible. This adds no colour.\nSCENE token: LittleCrab.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - ShoreCast   (bake SECOND, attach as @image2)\n\n🔴 SAME MEDIUM AS ABOVE - ink and gouache wash on cool grey-toned toothed paper, brush grain\n  visible, contours left open. Each of these appears on exactly ONE page, so each must be\n  recognisable from SILHOUETTE ALONE at thumbnail size.\n\nPERIWINKLE (p4): a small pointed grey-blue spiral snail clamped flat to the rock, only the lip\n  of its shell parted a crack to speak. Deadpan. It does not move and it has no visible body -\n  the comedy is that it obviously cannot have taken anything. Cool grey-blue wash, one darker\n  pass in the spiral groove. NO red.\nSHRIMP (p5): a near-translucent pale shrimp curled inside a puddle the size of a palm, with two\n  long feelers raised. Painted as almost bare paper with one faint cool wash and a few darker\n  strokes for the segments and the feelers - it must look like it is barely there, because it\n  barely has any water. NO red.\nOLD STARFISH (p6 only): five thick arms spread flat on the rock top, one arm slightly lifted.\n  🔴 Warm brick-red #C43A2B, painted OPAQUE and matte - the ONE fully saturated warm shape in\n  the whole book, and the only object anywhere that is not a thin wash. Slow, settled, old:\n  blunt arm tips, a coarse pebbled skin suggested by four or five dots, not a rendered texture.\n  🔴 Its red must never touch or tint anything else - not the rock under it, not the crab, not\n  the water. No warm glow spilling onto neighbours.\nREFERENCE SHEET: all three side by side at correct relative scale next to a small silhouette of\n  LittleCrab for size comparison; plus a close-up of the starfish arm tip showing the opaque\n  matte paint against a thin grey wash beside it. Plain cool grey paper background.\nSCENE tokens: Periwinkle, Shrimp, OldStarfish. Never write \"starfish\" alone."
